  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION FOR THE 00Z TAF PACKAGE...\n\nA rinse and repeat sort of day across the region today as low clouds \ndissipate this morning near the coast with a coastal eddy offshore. \nformed last night, bringing in low clouds to coastal areas this \nmorning. Temperatures will be more comfortable today with highs near \nnormal across the region. \n\nA weak troughing pattern over the West will be reinforced with \nanother shot of cooler air as another low pressure system moves \nsouthward Tuesday through Thursday. Pressure heights will continue \nfall, leading to a continued subtle cooling trend. Wednesday and \nThursday will see the coolest temperatures of the week. There is a \n75-100% chance of temperatures across the Inland Empire, Coachella \nValley and the large High Desert cities staying below 90 degrees \nthese days; plentiful 70s and 80s expected for areas along and west \nof the mountains. Low temperatures will also begin to cool off \nthrough the next few nights as well, with lows in the 50s and 60s \nalong and west of the mountains and in the high desert. Increased \nsea breeze winds will be felt along the desert slopes and into the \ndeserts each afternoon and evening through Thursday, some areas \ngusting over 35 MPH in the passes and High Desert.\n\nThe low pressure system to the north will move out by Friday into \nthe weekend. Models begin to diverge on what exact pressure systems \nmove over the area, but consensus continues to shift toward whatever \ndoes form will be weak in nature. This will lead to temperatures \nstaying near average for the latter part of the week with weaker \nwinds across the mountains and deserts.",
